import{_ as o,C as r,o as t,c as d,a3 as a,b as i,w as n,a as l,E as h,a4 as c}from"./chunks/framework.B02wg2PO.js";const v=JSON.parse('{"title":"Channels: Chat, Telegram & Email","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/channels.md","filePath":"concepts/channels.md"}'),p={name:"concepts/channels.md"};function u(g,e,m,b,f,k){const s=r("Mermaid");return t(),d("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=a(`<h1 id="channels-chat-telegram-email" tabindex="-1">Channels: Chat, Telegram &amp; Email <a class="header-anchor" href="#channels-chat-telegram-email" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Channels: Chat, Telegram &amp; Email&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A <strong>channel</strong> is a surface onto the same PRIME, the same runs, and the same memory. The dashboard is one; Telegram is another; transactional email is a one-way third. Nothing about the orchestrator changes per channel — an adapter owns only its wire protocol, and the shared chat engine owns the turn loop, the toolbelt, approvals, and the activity log.</p><p>For Telegram setup steps, see <a href="/docs/guides/telegram">Connecting Telegram</a>. This page is about how the machinery underneath works.</p><h2 id="the-adapter-contract" tabindex="-1">The adapter contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-adapter-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The adapter contract&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every messaging channel implements a deliberately small interface:</p><div class="language-ts vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ts</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">interface</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ChannelAdapter</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">  readonly</span><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;"> id</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ChannelId</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">;          </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">// &#39;telegram&#39; | &#39;discord&#39; | &#39;whatsapp&#39; | &#39;imessage&#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">  init</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">deps</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ChannelDeps</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> void</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> Promise</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">void</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">&gt;;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">  stop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> void</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Adapters register themselves at module load; <code>startChannels()</code> starts whatever is registered and never lets one adapter&#39;s failure stop the others, and <code>stopChannels()</code> tears them all down on shutdown. Only Telegram is implemented today — the other three ids exist as the stable keys for config and identity rows.</p><p><strong><code>startChannels()</code> is called only when the process is not in cloud mode.</strong> Channels run as the single fixed local tenant, which is why the sections below describe a flat allow-list rather than per-member identity.</p><h2 id="per-org-channel-config" tabindex="-1">Per-org channel config <a class="header-anchor" href="#per-org-channel-config" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Per-org channel config&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Channel configuration lives in one store shared by all channels, keyed by <code>(orgId, channel)</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>enabled</code></td><td>Whether the adapter should connect</td></tr><tr><td><code>secrets</code></td><td>Channel-specific credentials, e.g. <code>{ botToken }</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>publicCfg</code></td><td>Non-secret settings, e.g. <code>{ allowedUserIds }</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>webhookSecret</code></td><td>Minted on first save; identifies the org for an inbound webhook path</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Secrets are envelope-encrypted at rest whenever a master key is configured, and the UI view never receives a raw secret — only a <code>set</code> flag and a last-3 hint. Telegram additionally migrates the legacy <code>memory/telegram.json</code> (itself seeded once from <code>TELEGRAM_*</code> environment variables) into this store on first read, so an existing install keeps its token with no interruption. See <a href="/docs/reference/configuration">Configuration</a> for the environment variables involved.</p><h2 id="inbound-message-→-turn" tabindex="-1">Inbound: message → turn <a class="header-anchor" href="#inbound-message-→-turn" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Inbound: message → turn&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A chat message becomes one of two things, decided by whether the chat is <em>focused</em> on a run:</p>`,14)),(t(),i(c,null,{default:n(()=>[h(s,{id:"mermaid-83",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TD%0A%20%20%20%20IN(%5B%22Inbound%20message%5Cn(dashboard%20or%20Telegram)%22%5D)%20--%3E%20SL%7B%22starts%20with%20%2F%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20SL%20--%3E%7Cyes%7C%20CMD%5B%22Slash%20command%5Cnhandled%20inline%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20SL%20--%3E%7Cno%7C%20FOC%7B%22chat%20has%20a%5Cnfocused%20run%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20FOC%20--%3E%7Cyes%7C%20STR%5B%22sendToRun(runId%2C%20text%2C%20attachments)%5Cnreply%20via%20run-event%20relay%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20FOC%20--%3E%7Cno%7C%20Q%5B%22enqueueChatTurn(...)%5Cnon%20the%20main%20chat%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Q%20--%3E%20W%5B%22Per-(org%2C%20conversation)%20worker%5Cnturns%20serialize%3B%20different%20chats%20run%20in%20parallel%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20W%20--%3E%20REC%5B%22Written%20to%20ChatConversation%5CnBEFORE%20ack%20%E2%80%94%20survives%20a%20restart%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20REC%20--%3E%20RUN%5B%22PRIME's%20turn%3A%5CnonDelta%20%2F%20onToolStatus%20%2F%20onConfirm%20%2F%20onTurnEnd%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:n(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[l(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<p><strong>Focused</strong> means the chat has bound itself to one run (<code>/focus</code>, <code>/newrun</code>, or the Focus button). Non-command text and media then go <em>into</em> that run, and the reply comes back through the run-event relay rather than as a chat turn. Focus is cleared automatically if the run is gone or cancelled.</p><p><strong>Main-chat turns</strong> go through the durable chat engine. One worker exists per <code>(org, conversation)</code>, so turns in the same chat serialize across browser tabs and Telegram while different chats drain in parallel. An accepted turn is written onto the <code>ChatConversation</code> record <em>before</em> it is acknowledged, so it survives a restart:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th><code>PendingChatTurn</code> field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>id</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>attachments</code></td><td>The queued message</td></tr><tr><td><code>source</code></td><td><code>dashboard</code> | <code>telegram</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>channelChatId</code></td><td>Telegram destination, so the renderer can be reattached after a restart</td></tr><tr><td><code>userId</code></td><td>Acting member (cloud); absent for the local/system tenant</td></tr><tr><td><code>state</code></td><td><code>queued</code> | <code>running</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>At most 10 turns may wait; an eleventh is refused with a queue-full message rather than dropped. On boot, <code>resumePendingChatTurns()</code> requeues them — the dashboard&#39;s are recovered during server boot, Telegram&#39;s wait until the bot has reconnected so their replies have somewhere to go.</p><p>Attachments are handed to PRIME as absolute paths, not as inline content: files for the main chat land in the chat-uploads dir, files for a focused run land in that run&#39;s assets dir prefixed <code>up-</code> so the asset sweep skips them.</p><h2 id="outbound-the-channelui" tabindex="-1">Outbound: the ChannelUi <a class="header-anchor" href="#outbound-the-channelui" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Outbound: the ChannelUi&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An adapter implements one callback interface and gets a native rendering of a turn:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Callback</th><th>Fires when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>onTurnStart</code></td><td>The message left the FIFO and its turn is running</td></tr><tr><td><code>onDelta</code></td><td>PRIME streamed a chunk of reply text</td></tr><tr><td><code>onToolStatus</code></td><td>PRIME called a tool, or a delegated agent reported progress</td></tr><tr><td><code>onWorking</code></td><td>Heartbeat (every 3s) while blocked on a long tool call</td></tr><tr><td><code>onQueued</code></td><td>The message was queued behind an in-flight turn</td></tr><tr><td><code>onConfirm</code> / <code>onConfirmResolved</code></td><td>An approval card is raised, then settled</td></tr><tr><td><code>onError</code>, <code>onTurnEnd</code></td><td>Failure, and completion (including cancellation)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The <code>deliverFile</code> hook is what backs the <code>send_file</code> brain tool. It is optional: on the dashboard there is no chat to deliver into, so the tool reports that it only works in a chat channel rather than silently failing.</p><h2 id="telegram-connection" tabindex="-1">Telegram: connection <a class="header-anchor" href="#telegram-connection" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Telegram: connection&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The adapter long-polls; there is no webhook. On connect it calls <code>getMe</code> to validate the token, calls <code>deleteWebhook</code> (a stale webhook makes <code>getUpdates</code> return 409), registers the slash-command list, then loops <code>getUpdates</code> with a 50-second timeout and <code>allowed_updates: [&#39;message&#39;, &#39;callback_query&#39;]</code>.</p><p>The service can be reconfigured live from Settings with no restart:</p><ul><li>token or <code>enabled</code> changed → tear down the loop and start a fresh one;</li><li>only the allowed-ids changed → mutate the live allow-set in place.</li></ul><p>Applies are serialized, so rapid saves cannot interleave a stop with a start. Poll failures back off exponentially from 1s to a 30s ceiling. A 409 is surfaced verbatim in the connection status — <strong>one process at a time may poll a given bot token</strong>, so that error usually means a second instance is running. A 401 is reported as an invalid token. See <a href="/docs/operations/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> for the operational side.</p><h2 id="allowed-senders" tabindex="-1">Allowed senders <a class="header-anchor" href="#allowed-senders" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Allowed senders&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Authorization is a flat allow-list of Telegram user ids held as a mutable <code>Set</code>:</p><ul><li>only <strong>private</strong> chats are handled — group, supergroup, and channel updates are dropped, as are messages from other bots;</li><li>every sender is recorded (so Settings can list who has messaged the bot) <em>before</em> the authorization check;</li><li>an unauthorized sender gets exactly one reply, containing their own user id and instructions to authorize it from Settings; subsequent messages are silent, so an unknown sender cannot be used to generate traffic;</li><li>unauthorized callback-button presses are answered with &quot;Not authorized.&quot;</li></ul><p>Because the allow-list is global rather than per-member, every allow-listed account is the same local superadmin user. Run notices are broadcast to all of them. That is a known limitation of running channels local-only — see <a href="/docs/reference/orgs-auth-permissions">Orgs, Auth &amp; Permissions</a>.</p><h2 id="commands-and-the-runs-ui" tabindex="-1">Commands and the runs UI <a class="header-anchor" href="#commands-and-the-runs-ui" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Commands and the runs UI&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Commands are global: they work the same whether or not the chat is focused on a run.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Commands</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main chat</td><td><code>/clear</code>, <code>/new</code>, <code>/cancel</code>, <code>/status</code>, <code>/help</code>, <code>/id</code></td></tr><tr><td>Runs</td><td><code>/runs</code>, <code>/run [id]</code>, <code>/newrun &lt;brief&gt;</code>, <code>/focus &lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>/main</code></td></tr><tr><td>Approvals</td><td><code>/approve [note]</code>, <code>/deny [note]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Files</td><td><code>/files [id]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Notifications</td><td><code>/mute</code>, <code>/unmute</code></td></tr><tr><td>Discovery</td><td><code>/projects</code>, <code>/find &lt;text&gt;</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>/approve</code> and <code>/deny</code> target the focused run when it is gated, otherwise the first run anywhere with a pending approval.</p><p>Beyond commands, runs are driven by inline keyboards. Callback data is namespaced — <code>r:</code> for run actions, <code>cf:</code> for approval cards, <code>stop:</code> for cancelling a turn — so the button sets never collide. A run card shows status, project and branch, the first eight checklist items, and any approval gate; the run&#39;s (potentially long) last result is sent as its own chunked message rather than clipped into the card.</p><h2 id="run-notices-and-relays" tabindex="-1">Run notices and relays <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-notices-and-relays" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run notices and relays&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Two bridges connect the run store&#39;s live signals to Telegram:</p><ul><li><strong>Thread events</strong> → the chats currently <em>focused</em> on that run, so PRIME&#39;s replies and generated files stream in live. Operator echoes are skipped (they are already in the chat), and tool/result/delegation noise is filtered out.</li><li><strong>Lifecycle notices</strong> → every authorized, unmuted chat, labelled by kind (needs input, paused, done, error, cancelled) with an <strong>Open</strong> button. A run parked at an approval gate instead renders Approve/Deny buttons for that exact change.</li></ul><p>A chat already focused on a run is not interrupted by that run&#39;s lifecycle notices — it is watching the thread live — <em>unless</em> the notice is an actionable approval gate, which has no thread event of its own.</p><p>Both relays are fail-soft by construction: they run off store observers and must never throw, because a failure there cannot be allowed to break a store write. A &quot;typing…&quot; indicator is kept alive on a 4-second cadence while a focused run is active, since Telegram&#39;s typing action expires after about five seconds. See <a href="/docs/concepts/reflexes-and-events">Reflexes &amp; Events</a> for the other consumer of the same notice bus.</p><h2 id="formatting-and-streaming" tabindex="-1">Formatting and streaming <a class="header-anchor" href="#formatting-and-streaming" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Formatting and streaming&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Telegram cannot stream tokens, so the renderer simulates it: send one message immediately for instant feedback, then edit it as text accrues — throttled to 1.5s — with a live status footer and a ⏹ Stop button, and roll over to a fresh message as the text approaches the length ceiling.</p><p>PRIME&#39;s markdown is converted to Telegram&#39;s narrow HTML subset (<code>&lt;b&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;s&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;code&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code>); everything else is flattened to readable text. Headings become bold, bullets become <code>•</code>, horizontal rules become an em-dash run, and markdown tables — which Telegram cannot render — are preserved inside <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> so their alignment survives. Inline code spans are treated as opaque, so emphasis markers inside them are never mangled. An unfinished code fence mid-stream is auto-closed for that paint and reopened on the next one.</p><p>Two fallbacks make this robust rather than brittle: if Telegram rejects the generated HTML with a parse error, the message is re-sent as plain text and that message stays plain; and a &quot;message is not modified&quot; error is treated as success. Rendering never breaks a turn.</p><h2 id="files" tabindex="-1">Files <a class="header-anchor" href="#files" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Files&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>send_file</code> delivers a file from disk to the current chat. The path is resolved defensively: a bare filename resolves under the chat-uploads dir, anything else must resolve to an existing file under the data root. Telegram then chooses the wire form by extension and size — PNG/JPEG/WebP under the photo ceiling go as photos with an inline preview; SVG and GIF are deliberately excluded (Telegram rejects SVG as a photo, and sending a GIF as a photo would flatten the animation) and ride as documents.</p><h2 id="channel-specific-limits" tabindex="-1">Channel-specific limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#channel-specific-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Channel-specific limits&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Value</th><th>Where it bites</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Message text</td><td>4096 chars</td><td>Telegram&#39;s hard ceiling</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk split threshold</td><td>3500 chars of source markdown</td><td>Wide margin, since HTML tags don&#39;t count toward the visible limit</td></tr><tr><td>Caption</td><td>1024 chars</td><td>Captions, not message text — clipped with an ellipsis</td></tr><tr><td>Inbound file download</td><td>20 MB</td><td>Telegram&#39;s <code>getFile</code> ceiling for bots; larger files are refused with a message</td></tr><tr><td>Photo send</td><td>10 MB</td><td>Above this an image goes as a document</td></tr><tr><td>Document send</td><td>50 MB</td><td>Above this the send throws</td></tr><tr><td>Files per <code>/files</code></td><td>10</td><td>The remainder is reported, not sent</td></tr><tr><td>Chat queue</td><td>10 turns</td><td>Shared with the dashboard, per conversation</td></tr><tr><td>Streaming edit interval</td><td>1.5s</td><td>Flood-control margin</td></tr><tr><td>Media-group batching</td><td>800ms</td><td>Album messages are collected into one turn</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Rate limiting is handled inside the API client: a 429 with a retry-after of 30 seconds or less is waited out once and the call retried, for both JSON and multipart requests.</p><h2 id="the-mail-channel" tabindex="-1">The mail channel <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-mail-channel" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The mail channel&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mail is one-way and transactional — it carries links, not conversation. The driver is selected by <code>SAIDI_MAIL_DRIVER</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Driver</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>console</code> (default)</td><td>Logs the message and its link; local and dev never send email</td></tr><tr><td><code>resend</code></td><td>Posts to the Resend REST API using <code>SAIDI_RESEND_API_KEY</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>SAIDI_MAIL_FROM</code> sets the sender. An unset API key with the <code>resend</code> driver logs a warning and drops the message rather than throwing. Three messages exist, each sent as both HTML and plain text: <strong>verify your email</strong>, <strong>reset your password</strong>, and <strong>workspace invite</strong>. Every one of them carries a single-use token in the link — the tokens themselves are described in <a href="/docs/reference/orgs-auth-permissions">Orgs, Auth &amp; Permissions</a>.</p>',41))])}const w=o(p,[["render",u]]);export{v as __pageData,w as default};
